<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Stafford Bettesworth Haines</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1802">1802</a>–<a href="/years/1860">1860</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stafford">Stafford</a> Bettesworth <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haine">Haines</a> was a British naval officer who captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a> for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> in January 1839, after a dispute with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of Lahej over a plundered ship. He then governed it as political agent for fifteen years and built up the port, which had shrunk to a village. Dismissed in 1854 over a deficit in his accounts, he spent six years in prison in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a>.</p></div>
